Figure 4: Rest-frame UV luminosity vs R3 index (left panel) and stellar mass vs oxygen abundance (right panel) of LAP2 and the SALSA arc at $z=5.66$ compared to literature systems. The latter were chosen among known high-redshift ($z>3$) galaxies, focusing on low-luminosity ($\rm M_{UV}\gtrsim-19$) and low-metallicity systems. The sample is color-coded according to the methodology used to infer the metallicity (strong lines vs direct $\rm T_{e}$ methods, vs photometric candidates). It is further divided to visually separate the lowest metallicity sources, possibly consistent with being close to pristine (e.g., $\rm Z\lesssim 1\%\penalty\ Z_{\odot}$ or $\rm R3\lesssim 1$). We point out that the “strong line” sample is inhomogeneous, as different empirical calibrations are used: Sanders et al. (2024) for, e.g., LAP2; Isobe et al. (2026) for LATED-1;
Cloudy (Ferland et al., 2017) and BPASS (Stanway and Eldridge, 2023) photo-ionization models for AMORE6; Nakajima et al. (2022) for, e.g., GLIMPSE-D; Bagpipes (Carnall et al., 2019b) SED fitting for, e.g., EMPC-123650. Masses also may be inhomogeneous, as derived under different assumptions; we however point out that most of the sample assumes either a Kroupa (2001) or a Chabrier (2003) IMF.
The references for the literature data are: Rojas-Ruiz et al. (2026) (SALSA); Cai et al. (2025) (CR3); Li et al. (2026) (LATED-1); Trussler et al. (2026) (EMPC-123650); Morishita et al. (2025) (AMORE6); Caputi et al. (2026) (LRD-NOM); Vanzella et al. (2023); Nakajima et al. (2025) (LAP1 and LAP1B); Maiolino et al. (2026a) (A2744-QSO1); Übler et al. (2026) (Hebe); Mondal et al. (2025) (GN-HeII-J1236); Cai and Yu (2026) (CAPERS-39810); Vanzella et al. (2024); Bolamperti et al. (2026) (CA and T2c); Fujimoto et al. (2025a) (GLIMPSE-16043); Hsiao et al. (2025) (SAPPHIRES); Chemerynska et al. (2024) (UNCOVER); Nishigaki et al. (2025) (DREAMS); Willott et al. (2025) (CANUCS-A370-LAE); Korber et al. (2026) (GLIMPSE-D); Cullen et al. (2025) (EXCELS-63107); Mowla et al. (2024) (Firefly Sparkle); Tripodi et al. (2024) (CANUCS-LRD); Curti et al. (2025) (JADES-GS). Upper limits from the literature have been homogenized to the $3\sigma$ level whenever the original significance was reported; for pseudo-LRD-NOM the significance of the limit is not specified (Caputi et al., 2026).

